des and hands out the next wave of only the slices still uncovered (`--next-wave` plans one without a result). A result that covered a new slice spends no attempt; two results in a row with nothing new, or six failed rounds, print the `- **Not run:** …` line to paste into the report, and a result with nothing new first has its open slices cut in half. `keryx review slice --package <package>` already wrote one driver per reviewer x wave (`drivers/<reviewer>-w<n>.json`: slice ids, absolute slice paths, `result` path; the dispatcher adds `model`). Dispatch each wave in one batch, save each reply to its driver's `result`, and after each batch run `keryx review next --package <package>` (never `retry-plan` once per reviewer): it returns every reviewer's next wave at once, with reason `missing`, `retry`, `stalled` or `escalated`, or `done`. After a crash or timeout run it first: it collects late results from `<package>/raw/` and never re-hands a finished slice. Only the operator can accept a smaller set than that (`review_override_reviewers`, or `keryx review override-reviewers` in their terminal). An unfinished reviewer is never counted as a clean pass. **Persist every result, on every host.** On keryx shell pass each reviewer `save_result_to: <package>/raw/<reviewer>.txt`: the host writes its full final text there. Elsewhere the prompt tells the reviewer to write its whole reply to `<package>/raw/<reviewer>.txt` before it answers. Either way `review complete` has raw evidence after the transcript is cut, and you parse that file, never the tool output. Build `research.json` with `keryx review ledger build --package <package>` — never by hand: it reads the saved results, `scope-files.json` and the retry state, marks a reviewer complete only when its result is finished and its payloads cover the scope, and prints each gap with the exact next command and the waves left (a `retry-plan --next-wave` call, or a `--dispositions` entry for a raw finding). Run `keryx review ingest